WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.772
Picture a stacked lunchbox, the kind with tiers that click together.

2
00:00:06.772 --> 00:00:09.647
In the bottom tier, you put a heavy jar of soup.

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:11.477
Then you change your mind.

4
00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:15.192
So you add a tier on top, with a sticker that says: no soup.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.822
Your friend lifts the box and frowns.

6
00:00:17.822 --> 00:00:19.787
It is just as heavy as before.

7
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.082
Silly with soup.

8
00:00:21.082 --> 00:00:25.882
But software images work exactly this way, and smart people get caught.

9
00:00:25.882 --> 00:00:27.302
So, today's question.

10
00:00:27.302 --> 00:00:30.480
If I delete a file, does the image get smaller?

11
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.380
Last time, we treated an image as one sealed box.

12
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:41.374
Take python 3.14 slim, a base image with Debian Linux and Python inside.

13
00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:44.205
It unpacks to about 135 megabytes.

14
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:50.743
If it really were one big archive, every small change to your app would mean shipping all of it again.

15
00:00:50.743 --> 00:00:53.897
And ten Python images would store Python ten times.

16
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.243
Wouldn't it be nice to ship and store only what changed?

17
00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:00.856
So an image is really a stack of tiers, called layers.

18
00:01:00.856 --> 00:01:05.062
Each layer is a set of file changes, compared with the layer below.

19
00:01:05.062 --> 00:01:10.649
For this image: Debian files at the bottom, then the Python build, then two small layers.

20
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:15.643
Images are built from a Dockerfile, a recipe with one step per line.

21
00:01:15.643 --> 00:01:20.068
A step that changes files, like RUN or COPY, makes a new layer.

22
00:01:20.068 --> 00:01:25.265
A step that only changes settings, like CMD or ENV, makes no layer at all.

23
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:31.769
Every layer is named by its fingerprint, a sha256 digest computed from its bytes.

24
00:01:31.769 --> 00:01:35.638
The same content gets the same name, on every machine.

25
00:01:35.638 --> 00:01:38.360
Change one byte, and the name changes.

26
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:45.977
So now build two images, both starting from python 3.14 slim, each adding one small file.

27
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.446
Docker lists each one at about 178 megabytes.

28
00:01:49.446 --> 00:01:53.342
But the bottom four layers have the same fingerprints.

29
00:01:53.342 --> 00:01:55.073
So they are stored once.

30
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.157
Only 16.84 kilobytes of each image are its own.

31
00:01:59.157 --> 00:02:02.193
The other 178.3 megabytes are shared.

32
00:02:02.193 --> 00:02:05.475
Pulls and pushes skip shared layers too.

33
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.097
Real lunchboxes cannot share a tier.

34
00:02:08.097 --> 00:02:11.559
Images can, because the name comes from the content.

35
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:14.466
Every layer so far is read-only.

36
00:02:14.466 --> 00:02:20.856
When a container starts, Docker adds one thin writable tier on top, for that container alone.

37
00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:24.901
The container sees all the tiers merged into one folder tree.

38
00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:29.741
To read a file, it looks from the top down, and uses the first copy it finds.

39
00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:33.643
Now the app changes a file that came from the image.

40
00:02:33.643 --> 00:02:39.873
The first write copies the whole file up into the container's tier, and later writes change that copy.

41
00:02:39.873 --> 00:02:41.644
That is called copy-on-write.

42
00:02:41.867 --> 00:02:44.129
The original below never changes.

43
00:02:44.129 --> 00:02:46.872
From above, the copy on top simply wins.

44
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.141
Now the soup.

45
00:02:49.141 --> 00:02:52.182
Start from Alpine, a tiny Linux image.

46
00:02:52.182 --> 00:02:55.384
One RUN step creates a 50 megabyte file.

47
00:02:55.384 --> 00:02:57.705
The next RUN step deletes it.

48
00:02:57.933 --> 00:02:59.192
The file is gone.

49
00:02:59.192 --> 00:03:02.080
Nobody inside the container can see it.

50
00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.858
So how big is the image?

51
00:03:03.858 --> 00:03:05.043
Pause and guess.

52
00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:10.380
Remember the rule: a layer can only add changes.

53
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.829
So the delete step adds a tiny marker, called a whiteout, that hides the file below.

54
00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:19.916
The 50 megabytes stay in their layer, and ship with every pull.

55
00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:26.113
Compare the same work done in one RUN step, creating and deleting joined with and and.

56
00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:27.254
Weigh both.

57
00:03:27.254 --> 00:03:30.185
The two-step image: 53.8 megabytes.

58
00:03:30.185 --> 00:03:32.195
The one-step image: 3.8.

59
00:03:32.195 --> 00:03:37.219
About fourteen times bigger, for a file nobody can even see.

60
00:03:37.433 --> 00:03:39.061
The sticker weighs nothing.

61
00:03:39.061 --> 00:03:41.351
The soup still weighs fifty megabytes.

62
00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:43.238
So here is the rule.

63
00:03:43.238 --> 00:03:46.323
A delete in a later step only hides the file.

64
00:03:46.323 --> 00:03:48.105
Clean up in the same step.

65
00:03:48.105 --> 00:03:53.179
Or, as we will see later, copy only the results into a fresh, clean stage.

66
00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.559
A tool called dive makes the waste visible.

67
00:03:56.559 --> 00:03:59.056
It walks the image layer by layer.

68
00:03:59.056 --> 00:04:05.667
For the two-step image it said: efficiency 14 percent, 50 megabytes wasted in big dot bin.

69
00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:06.035
Fail.

70
00:04:06.867 --> 00:04:07.520
Why care?

71
00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.860
Every deploy and every CI run pulls the image.

72
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.111
It takes disk on every machine.

73
00:04:13.111 --> 00:04:16.813
And every extra package is one more thing to patch.

74
00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:19.617
And the writable tier has its own limits.

75
00:04:19.617 --> 00:04:23.902
Copying a big file up is slow, and the tier dies with the container.

76
00:04:23.902 --> 00:04:28.061
That is why databases keep their files somewhere else, in volumes.

77
00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:30.571
So, the moral of the story.

78
00:04:30.571 --> 00:04:34.782
An image is a stack of read-only layers, shared by fingerprint.

79
00:04:34.782 --> 00:04:37.589
A container adds one writable tier on top.

80
00:04:37.589 --> 00:04:41.465
And a delete in a later layer is a sticker, not a removal.

81
00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:42.887
Quick check.

82
00:04:42.887 --> 00:04:46.922
One step downloads a big file, and a later step deletes it.

83
00:04:46.922 --> 00:04:48.359
Did the image shrink?

84
00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:50.779
No.

85
00:04:50.779 --> 00:04:53.182
The bytes stay in the lower layer.

86
00:04:53.182 --> 00:04:55.091
The delete only hides them.

87
00:04:55.091 --> 00:04:56.929
Clean up in the same step.
